---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="strf-main-content">
    <section class="strf-section">
      <h1>Exports Usage</h1>
      <p>
        Storagefy provides a modular export system for maximum flexibility.
        Whether you're managing state across React, Vue (Pinia), or Svelte — or
        choosing between
        <code>localStorage</code>, <code>sessionStorage</code>, or <code
          >IndexedDB</code
        > — the exported modules are designed to be highly composable and framework-agnostic.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="strf-section">
      <h2>1. Importing Adapters</h2>
      <p>
        You can directly import individual storage and framework adapters for
        advanced use cases or custom integrations.
      </p>
      <pre class="strf-code-block">
        <code>
import &#123; IndexedDBAdapter &#125; from "storagefy";
import &#123; LocalStorageAdapter &#125; from "storagefy";
import &#123; SessionStorageAdapter &#125; from "storagefy";

import &#123; PiniaAdapter &#125; from "storagefy";
import &#123; ReactAdapter &#125; from "storagefy";
import &#123; SvelteAdapter &#125; from "storagefy";
        </code>
      </pre>
    </section>

    <section class="strf-section">
      <h2>2. Using <code>startStoragefy</code></h2>
      <p>
        This function initializes and returns a storage adapter instance based
        on the provided configuration. You can reuse this adapter across
        frameworks.
      </p>
      <pre class="strf-code-block">
        <code>
import &#123; startStoragefy &#125;  from "storagefy";
const adapter = startStoragefy(&#123;
  dbName: "my-app",
  adapter: "indexedDB",          // the default; or "localStorage" or "sessionStorage"
  encrypt: true,                 // makes the values hard to read — not a way to keep a secret
  password: "my-app-secret",     // the encryption keys are derived from it
  keyDerivation: "hkdf",         // the default; "pbkdf2" only for a password the person types
  expireCheckInterval: 2000,     // ms; 0 turns the expiry timer off
  channelName: "storagefy-sync", // the channel the tabs talk on…
  enableSyncTabs: true,          // …and this storage tells them about its writes
&#125;);
        </code>
      </pre>
      <p>
        If an adapter is already started, the same instance is returned — unless <code>forceRecreate: true</code>
        (a new adapter replaces it: the old one is destroyed, and the store adapters kept over it stop keeping their
        stores — keep them again) or <code>fresh: true</code> (a new adapter is returned and the one started stays).
      </p>
      <p>
        Sync between tabs needs three settings: <code>channelName</code> and <code>enableSyncTabs</code> on the storage
        adapter, and <code>syncTabs: true</code> on each store that should follow the other tabs (see below). With
        only <code>channelName</code>, nothing is synced.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="strf-section">
      <h2>3. Retrieving Current Adapter</h2>
      <p>
        Once initialized with <code>startStoragefy</code>, you can retrieve the
        current adapter via:
      </p>
      <pre class="strf-code-block">
        <code>
import &#123; getStorageAdapter &#125; from "storagefy";          
const current = getStorageAdapter();
        </code>
      </pre>
    </section>

    <section class="strf-section">
      <h2>4. Ignoring Keys from Persistence</h2>
      <p>
        The <code>ignoreKeys</code> option lets you exclude sensitive or transient
        fields from being persisted. It supports both flat and nested keys using
        dot notation.
      </p>

      <h3>Flat keys</h3>
      <p>Exclude top-level properties by name:</p>
      <pre class="strf-code-block">
        <code>
await setInStorage(myStore, "user", &#123;
  ignoreKeys: ["password", "token"]
&#125;);

// Given state:
// &#123; name: "Alice", password: "secret", token: "abc123" &#125;

// Stored as (the ignored keys are left out):
// &#123; name: "Alice" &#125;
        </code>
      </pre>

      <h3>Nested keys (dot notation)</h3>
      <p>
        Exclude deeply nested properties using dot-separated paths. This works
        at any depth without affecting sibling or parent keys.
      </p>
      <pre class="strf-code-block">
        <code>
await setInStorage(myStore, "user", &#123;
  ignoreKeys: ["credentials.password", "credentials.token"]
&#125;);

// Given state:
// &#123;
//   name: "Alice",
//   credentials: &#123;
//     password: "secret",
//     token: "abc123",
//     role: "admin"
//   &#125;
// &#125;

// Stored as:
// &#123;
//   name: "Alice",
//   credentials: &#123;
//     role: "admin"  // unaffected
//   &#125;
// &#125;
        </code>
      </pre>

      <h3>Deep nesting</h3>
      <p>Paths can go as deep as needed:</p>
      <pre class="strf-code-block">
        <code>
await setInStorage(myStore, "app", &#123;
  ignoreKeys: ["config.api.secretKey", "config.api.privateToken"]
&#125;);

// Given state:
// &#123;
//   config: &#123;
//     api: &#123;
//       secretKey: "key-999",
//       privateToken: "tok-xyz",
//       baseUrl: "https://api.example.com"
//     &#125;
//   &#125;
// &#125;

// Stored as:
// &#123;
//   config: &#123;
//     api: &#123;
//       baseUrl: "https://api.example.com"  // unaffected
//     &#125;
//   &#125;
// &#125;
        </code>
      </pre>

      <h3>Mixing flat and nested keys</h3>
      <p>You can freely combine both styles in the same array:</p>
      <pre class="strf-code-block">
        <code>
await setInStorage(myStore, "user", &#123;
  ignoreKeys: [
    "sessionToken",             // flat key
    "profile.password",         // nested key
    "profile.security.pin"      // deeply nested key
  ]
&#125;);
        </code>
      </pre>
    </section>

    <section class="strf-section">
      <h2>5. Framework Adapters</h2>

      <div class="strf-adapter">
        <h3>React</h3>
        <p>
          To use Storagefy with Zustand, Redux, Jotai, or custom React stores:
        </p>
        <pre class="strf-code-block">
          <code>
import &#123; getReactAdapter &#125; from "storagefy";
// Over a new localStorage adapter (or over the one startStoragefy() started, without adapterParams)
const react = getReactAdapter(&#123;
  adapterParams: &#123; adapter: "localStorage", dbName: "my-react-app" &#125;
&#125;);

await react.getFromStorage(myStore, "user"); // load what was kept — before setInStorage, which writes the state

await react.setInStorage(myStore, "user", &#123;
  timeout: 3600000,
  ignoreKeys: [
    "tempId",               // flat key
    "auth.password",        // nested key
    "auth.session.token"    // deeply nested key
  ],
  syncTabs: true
&#125;);
          </code>
        </pre>
        <p>Or use the helper:</p>
        <pre class="strf-code-block">
          <code>
// startStoragefy() must be called first
await setReactStorage(myStore, "settings", &#123;
  timeout: 300000,
  ignoreKeys: ["_temp", "loading", "auth.password"],
  syncTabs: true
&#125;);
          </code>
        </pre>
      </div>

      <div class="strf-adapter">
        <h3>Vue (Pinia)</h3>
        <p>Get a preconfigured Pinia adapter using:</p>
        <pre class="strf-code-block">
          <code>
// startStoragefy() must be called first
const &#123; setInStorage, getFromStorage &#125; = getPiniaAdapter(&#123;
  adapterParams: &#123; adapter: "sessionStorage", dbName: "my-vue-app" &#125;
&#125;);

await getFromStorage(myStore, "user"); // load what was kept — before setInStorage, which writes the state

await setInStorage(myStore, "user", &#123;
  timeout: 3600000,
  ignoreKeys: [
    "tempId",               // flat key
    "auth.password",        // nested key
    "auth.session.token"    // deeply nested key
  ],
  syncTabs: true
&#125;);
          </code>
        </pre>
        <p>Or use the helper:</p>
        <pre class="strf-code-block">
          <code>
await setPiniaStorage(myStore, "user", &#123;
  timeout: 3600000,
  ignoreKeys: ["tempId", "auth.password"],
  syncTabs: true
&#125;);
          </code>
        </pre>
      </div>

      <div class="strf-adapter">
        <h3>Svelte</h3>
        <p>Integrate Svelte <code>writable</code> stores:</p>
        <pre class="strf-code-block">
          <code>
const svelte = getSvelteAdapter(&#123;
  adapterParams: &#123; adapter: "indexedDB", dbName: "my-svelte-app" &#125;
&#125;);

await svelte.getFromStorage(myStore, "theme"); // load what was kept — before setInStorage, which writes the state

await svelte.setInStorage(myStore, "theme", &#123;
  timeout: 86400000,
  ignoreKeys: [
    "preview",              // flat key
    "user.password",        // nested key
    "user.api.secretKey"    // deeply nested key
  ],
  syncTabs: true
&#125;);
          </code>
        </pre>
        <p>Or simply use the shorthand:</p>
        <pre class="strf-code-block">
          <code>
// startStoragefy() must be called first            
await setSvelteStorage(myStore, "theme", &#123;
  timeout: 86400000,
  ignoreKeys: ["preview", "user.password"],
  syncTabs: true
&#125;);
          </code>
        </pre>
      </div>

      <p>All <code>set*</code> functions accept the same parameters:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>store</code>: The store instance you want to persist</li>
        <li><code>key</code>: A string key under which to store the data</li>
        <li>
          <code>options</code> (optional):
          <ul>
            <li>
              <code>timeout</code>: <strong>expiration</strong> of the stored state, in milliseconds after each
              write — not a time limit of the operation: after it, the stored state is gone. A change of an
              <code>ignoreKeys</code> key alone writes nothing, so it does not renew it
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>ignoreKeys</code>: Array of keys to exclude from persistence.
              Supports flat keys (<code>"password"</code>) and dot-notation paths
              for nested keys (<code>"auth.password"</code>, <code>"auth.api.token"</code>).
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>syncTabs</code>: If <code>true</code>, this store applies the changes of the other tabs — the
              storage adapter needs <code>channelName</code> and <code>enableSyncTabs</code>. Only a state newer
              than the store's is applied (every write has a revision); with IndexedDB and localStorage, a tab shown
              again reads storage again
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>onError</code>: called with an <code>Error</code> when the state could not be saved (storage
              full…) or a change from another tab could not be read. <code>getFromStorage(store, key, &#123; onError
              &#125;)</code> also tells a stored state that cannot be read
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="strf-section">
      <h2>6. Exported API Summary</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>IndexedDBAdapter</code>, <code>LocalStorageAdapter</code>, <code
            >SessionStorageAdapter</code
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>PiniaAdapter</code>, <code>ReactAdapter</code>, <code
            >SvelteAdapter</code
          >
        </li>
        <li><code>startStoragefy(config)</code></li>
        <li><code>getStorageAdapter()</code></li>
        <li>
          <code>getPiniaAdapter()</code>, <code>getReactAdapter()</code>, <code
            >getSvelteAdapter()</code
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>setPiniaStorage()</code>, <code>setReactStorage()</code>, <code
            >setSvelteStorage()</code
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>